; A `match` is exhaustiveness-checked by the matrix walk only when the
; scrutinee's type names a declared `data`. The AX3005 backstop that
; catches everything else additionally required that NO arm be a
; constructor - "no arm is a constructor and nothing catches the rest"
; - and those two conditions between them left a hole neither covered:
; a match whose arms ARE constructors, over a scrutinee whose type the
; checker cannot name. Both checks were off at once, which is the only
; combination that is unsound.
;
; The shapes that land there are not exotic. `ctorChecked` is 1 only
; for a TAG_T_CON naming a known `data`, so a lambda parameter (bound
; at a fresh `_tN`), any result of a polymorphic function - `vecGet`,
; `mapGet`, a user `(-> a a)` - and any macro spine all fell outside
; it. Measured, with a compiler from before 2026-08-17:
;
;   (fn (pick s) (match s ((Circle r) "circle") ((Sq w) "square")))
;
; over `(idf (Tri 3))` checked clean and answered a null `String`;
; `strLen` on it was exit 139 with no output.
;
; The first attempt at this widened the FALL-THROUGH backstop to cover
; the case, and that was wrong in a way worth recording: it refused 8
; correct programs out of 30 written in stdlib idiom - `(match (vecGet
; v i) ((Circle r) ..) ((Square w) ..))` and the same through
; `mapGet`, a lambda parameter, an effect handler's command, a user
; `(-> a a)` - because enumerating every constructor is not something
; the backstop can see. Worse, the `_` arm the diagnostic asked for
; would have turned a total match into a silent 0 at exit 0, which is
; the very outcome MM-VAL-9b names. A check whose suggested fix makes
; the program less correct is not a conservative check.
;
; What runs instead is the MATRIX walk, seeded from the constructors
; the ARMS name when the scrutinee's type cannot be named. So the case
; below reports `missing Tri` - the real answer - rather than a
; fall-through warning, and the eight programs are accepted again.
; The backstop still covers what the matrix cannot reach: arms that
; name no constructor at all.
;
; The second half is the `true`/`false` exemption asked of the
; SCRUTINEE. Both literals present is an exhaustiveness argument only
; when the thing matched is a `Bool`; over an `Int` they are ordinary
; value tests covering 1 and 0, and everything else falls through.

(data Shape (Circle Int) (Sq Int) (Tri Int))

(:: idf (-> a a))
(fn (idf x) x)

; Constructor arms, scrutinee type unknown: neither check ran.
(:: viaPoly (-> Int Int))
(fn (viaPoly n)
  (let ((s (idf (Tri n))))
    (match s
      ((Circle r) r)
      ((Sq w) w))))

; `true`/`false` over a non-Bool scrutinee: 7 is neither.
(:: viaBoolArms (-> Int Int))
(fn (viaBoolArms n)
  (match n
    ((true) 1)
    ((false) 2)))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) (+ (viaPoly 3) (viaBoolArms 7)))
